findings.explanation:
The client email (item viii) asks for a bona fide retention carve-out to be added. Section 5.2(i) and (ii) (p68, p69) and Section 5.3 (p71) are unchanged from v.6.2. Between them they already cover:
- automated backup and archival copies;
- copies retained under legal, regulatory or professional requirements;
- continued confidentiality for the Section 11 period.

This appears to satisfy S-09 §9.3, so no new carve-out seems necessary. The lawyer should confirm the existing wording meets the client's intent. The carve-out lasts 'for the full duration specified in Section 11', so it depends on the outcome at p111.

The renumbering follows from deleting the residuals section. If residuals are restored (S-06 Hard Line; client email item v), this heading and every downstream reference must revert. Those references are:
- 6.1/6.2/6.3 in p80, p81 and p85;
- the 'Section 6.1' cross-references in p81 and p83;
- p112;
- the proposed standstill 'Section 7' in p86, p95 and p113.

The paragraph also carries visible track-change markers in its text.

The v.6.2 form has no standstill. S-11 §11.1–11.2 says any standstill proposal requires escalation and there is no attorney-level acceptance tier. Under the S-11 §11.4 Hard Lines, this proposal fails on three counts:
- it runs 12 months (over the 6-month maximum);
- it includes a don't-ask-don't-waive clause;
- it has no third-party-bid fall-away.

The client email (item x) instructs outright rejection. The 6-month / fall-away / no-DADW fallback is held in reserve and is not to be offered in the first instance. Relevant paragraphs are p86–p95 and survival clause p113.

Corrigan made three changes:
- deleted 'without the requirement of posting any bond, surety, or other security';
- deleted the Receiving Party's bond waiver;
- added a $500,000 bond precondition.

S-14 §14.3 and S-10 §10.4(c) reject any bond requirement as Hard Lines. The client email (item xii) instructs that injunctive relief be available without bond.

The proviso refers to a 'tribunal', which interacts with the arbitration proposal (p121). The suggested text restores the v.6.2 Section 8.2 wording without the stray bold markup.
